# Template for ~/.config/security-scan/disclosure-patterns
#
# tools/publish-public.sh refuses to publish if any of these matches anywhere
# in the tree. One POSIX extended regular expression per line; '#' starts a
# comment; matching is case-insensitive.
#
#   cp examples/disclosure-patterns.example \
#      ~/.config/security-scan/disclosure-patterns
#   chmod 600 ~/.config/security-scan/disclosure-patterns
#   $EDITOR ~/.config/security-scan/disclosure-patterns
#
# Your real list belongs in that file and NOT in this repository -- a list of
# the names you must never publish is itself a list of those names. Everything
# below is a placeholder; replace it.
#
# The only string exempted from the scan is the public repository's own name
# (the value of --repo), because it legitimately appears in every `uses:` ref.

# --- organisations, clients, project or repository names ---
example-corp
example-client
acme-
proj-

# --- people: account names, email domains, surnames ---
@example\.com
example-user
jdoe

# --- attacker identifiers ---
#
# If an intrusion reached you through a compromised contributor account, the
# account name usually belongs to a victim rather than to the operator.
# Publishing it accuses someone of an intrusion they were the first casualty
# of, and detects nothing an operator cannot change in a minute. Keep the
# identifiers here so they are never published, and feed them to the scanner
# at run time with `scan.py --actors FILE` when you need to hunt for them.
some-hijacked-account
